============================================================
9. CURRENT WARNINGS / ITEMS TO VERIFY
============================================================

1. Cache state after physical-page replacement

   The old PTE and TLB entry are invalidated when a physical frame is
   reassigned, but old L1/L2 entries associated with that frame may
   remain valid. Final cache-invalidation behaviour should be confirmed.

2. Virtually-tagged L1 across process contexts

   Two PIDs can use the same VA while mapping it to different PFNs.
   The TLB distinguishes them correctly, but the current L1 virtual
   tag does not contain PID/context information. The expected
   context-switch/cache policy should be confirmed.

3. L2 LRU-counter representation

   The L2 test chooses the expected victim and dirty writeback path.
   The final counter-state dump should still be manually checked
   against the exact LRU-counter convention taught in class.

4. Course-specific semantics still to confirm

   - exact meaning of look-through
   - exact meaning of look-aside
   - L2 tag/address convention
   - write-allocate versus no-write-allocate
   - full-TLB replacement policy


============================================================
10. KNOWN LIMITATIONS
============================================================

- main.c currently uses deterministic demonstration addresses rather
  than a final external workload parser.

- Some lower/upper resident-page limits used in tests are test values
  and must be replaced by actual assignment/workload values if a final
  input specification provides them.

- The simulator models hierarchy state and control flow, not clock- or
  signal-level CPU/cache timing.

- Cache data arrays exist structurally, but current validation focuses
  primarily on address mapping, hit/miss behaviour, residency,
  replacement and write policy.

- The WARN conditions above are intentionally retained until the exact
  course interpretation is confirmed.


