- L2 hits/misses
- L2 dirty writebacks


============================================================
12. IMPORTANT VIVA POINTS
============================================================

- TLB comparisons are parallel in real hardware but are emulated using
  a sequential C scan.

- Address decomposition uses masks and shifts to mirror selection of
  address-bus fields.

- L1 is virtually tagged and physically indexed, so both VA and PA are
  involved in an L1 lookup.

- Page replacement and cache replacement are separate:
      Main memory -> global page-level LRU
      L1          -> square-matrix LRU
      L2          -> LRU counter

- The first two pages are pre-paged only into main memory. They are not
  inserted into the TLB or caches before execution.

- A TLB miss does not necessarily imply a page fault.

- A TLB hit does not necessarily imply a cache hit.

- L1 is write-through, therefore writes continue downward.

- L2 is write-back, therefore modified L2 lines can remain dirty until
  eviction.

- Global page replacement may choose a page belonging to another
  process as long as lower/upper resident-page constraints remain
  satisfied.


